;
;  NOTE ON PLACEMENT: this block is LAST simply to keep a 14,000-line vendored
;  library out of the way of the code that is actually maintained here. It is
;  NOT load-bearing: `#Warn` is not positional (AutoHotkey applies it to the
;  whole script wherever it appears), which is exactly why modification 1
;  below switches it off rather than relying on section order.
;
;  NOTE ON OMISSIONS: core/Worker.ahk is NOT bundled. It is a standalone
;  worker PROCESS that GpGFX launches as its own script file, it carries
;  `#SingleInstance Off` (which would override RadMapper's `Force` and allow
;  two engines to hook the mouse at once), and nothing RadMapper does uses
;  the worker pool.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MODIFICATIONS -- SEVEN, all marked in place. Nothing else is changed.
;
;  1. `#Warn All` -> `#Warn All, Off` (a few lines below). It is a development
;     aid; AutoHotkey applies the directive across the whole script, so left
;     on it raises modal warning dialogs about RadMapper's own code --
;     unacceptable on a reading workstation.
;
;  2. `Layer_WindowProc` now converts WM_MOUSEWHEEL's lParam from SCREEN to
;     CLIENT coordinates before hit-testing (search "RadMapper modification
;     #2"). WM_MOUSEWHEEL is the one message in the library's msgEvents map
;     that carries screen coordinates; the others carry client coordinates,
;     and the hit test compares against layer-local shape bounds. Without the
;     conversion no shape is ever found under a wheel event, so no
;     MouseScrollUp / MouseScrollDown handler fires and nothing in the UI
;     scrolls unless its layer happens to sit at the top-left of the primary
;     monitor. This is an upstream bug, not a RadMapper preference.
;
;  3. `LayerStack.ActiveLayer` no longer hands out, or dereferences, a pointer
;     to a layer that has been disposed (search "RadMapper modification #3"),
;     and `PopHistory` no longer falls back to an unchecked history entry.
;     activePtr is a RAW pointer -- the stack holds no reference to the layer
;     it names -- and the original getter passed it to ObjFromPtrAddRef
;     unchecked while the setter immediately read value.w/.h/.x/.y off it. So
;     the moment activePtr outlived its layer, the ordinary
;     save-active-layer / restore-active-layer idiom (about sixty places here,
;     and inside GpGFX itself) became "Critical Error: Invalid memory
;     read/write" -- which AutoHotkey does not let you catch, so it takes the
;     script down. Reported from TeleportSignal in v0.4.1. Upstream bug.
;
;  4. `Layer_WindowProc` re-arms TrackMouseEvent on EVERY WM_MOUSEMOVE
;     (search "RadMapper modification #4"). TME_LEAVE is a ONE-SHOT request.
;     The original armed it only while a `_trackingMouse` flag was false and
;     cleared that flag only inside the WM_MOUSELEAVE handler, so any leave
;     that never arrived -- the window hidden and reshown, the cursor leaving
;     during a modal drag or a blocking field-edit loop, a leave posted to a
;     window already destroyed -- left tracking unarmed and MouseLeave never
;     fired again for the life of the layer. Every hover highlight the
;     pointer touched stayed lit. Re-arming is idempotent (the call replaces
;     the pending request rather than stacking) and costs one DllCall per
;     move. Reported in v0.5.0. Upstream bug.
;
;  5. RadMapper-owned layers can set `alwaysFullErase`; `Draw` then clears the
;     whole backing surface instead of the prior dirty bounds. GpGFX does not
;     clip Text, so text overflow can live outside those bounds and ghost.
;
;  6. `Layer_WindowProc` routes shape events through `RadUiCall` instead of
;     bare `try`. The handler still cannot break the message pump, but its
;     error is now visible in RadMapper Diagnostics and OutputDebug.
;
